From a51042c864424650bed9d5c5aab02bdb4da51461 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 21:34:10 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 08/13] hv: vmbus: retain buffer ownership across GPADL and
 page state

A ring or data buffer can reach vmbus_free_buffer() while the host
still holds the pages, while the GPADL teardown has not confirmed, or
while the page encryption state is still unresolved. Freeing in that
window drops host-visible pages back into the page allocator with the
wrong encryption state and no proof that the host has released them.

Introduce struct vmbus_buffer_retained. Every allocation is tracked as
an owner record keyed by channel lifetime_id, and is freed only after
every gate has cleared: the GPADL is gone, the host has been revoked
or never owned the pages, the encryption state is known, and no
mapping reference remains. The owner leaves the global list only when
vmbus_buffer_owner_can_reclaim() agrees.

The reclaim path is a delayed work item on a dedicated
vmbus-buffer-reclaim workqueue. vmbus_release_buffer() marks the owner
released and schedules the reclaim. A host rescind walks the channel
through vmbus_buffer_revoke_channel() with its lifetime_id, so only
that channel's owners are marked host-revoked. The workqueue is torn
down in vmbus_exit() through vmbus_buffer_reclaimer_shutdown() so a
module unload cannot leave a pending timer behind.

Three encryption facts travel with the owner: needs_encrypt for pages
allocated decrypted that must be re-encrypted before free,
raw_decrypted for a temporary decryption spanning the GPADL post, and
encryption_unknown when neither state can be proven. An owner in the
unknown state is permanently leaked rather than freed into the wrong
state.

The allocation and GPADL paths grow _owned entry points that take a
struct vmbus_buffer and keep the owner attached to the caller's
buffer. The existing vmbus_alloc_buffer() and
vmbus_establish_gpadl_caller_decrypted() prototypes are unchanged;
each wraps its _owned form and detaches the owner before returning.

Add five KUnit cases to vmbus_buffer_test.c covering the reclaim
gate, the schedule gate, host-revoke state transitions, mapping
reference counting, and repeated release of one owner. The helpers
they need are declared in hyperv_vmbus.h and stay unexported; the
tests are built into the hv_vmbus object with the other cases.

Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
